s in the United  States as new immigrants I think the  fact that they came here means they're  risk takers to begin with they're  accustomed to the idea of moving they  don't find it some unthinkable shock  obviously they don't necessarily have  strong ties to the first part of the  United States they've come to settle in  and if someone says hey there's a better  job for you in Chicago I think their  attitude is why not let's try it they  can always go back say to Mexico or  Central America that's a very healthy  attitude when it comes to political  decisions that are made by whoever's in  the White House whoever's in charge in  the House of Representatives or the  Senate are the differences between  Democrats and Republicans in terms of  how they will impact this broader stasis  and complacency even noticeable or is  this an area in which all of our elected  officials are quite similar in terms of  whether they would even change the  status quo you described I think in a  funny way they're quite similar so  anyone who watches politics you see at  the rhetorical level there has not been  so much polarization for a long time  what the Trump people say the Clinton  people say it all sounds so horrible  they attack each other but when you get  right down to what's likely to happen  with the budget say the failure of the  Republicans to roll back Obamacare  whether you agree with that or not I  think we see we're living in a time of  stasis where not many things change and  on fundamentals there's a lot more  agreement across the two parties we're  actually just not really going to do  very much the visions of Trump make  America great again very backward  looking calorie Clinton was backward  looking to the 90s into the Obama  administration a big contrast with say  JFK who talked all about the new  frontier what's an example of the type  of policy that a president could  actually possibly propose and effect  that would change the direction that you  identify in the book more money for  research and development with our  infrastructure to get that moving again  and I don't just mean repairing roads  and tunnels so that's fine but to do  something like help implement the smart  grid or introduce more online education  we need to make it much easier for poor  people to move into cities so deregulate  building in those cities to some extent  there's a lot we could do I think the  key point is neither party seems that  interested in actually doing it's a fair  amount of talk that very little action  on either side yeah that may be I mean  at the same time what you're talking  about  it there's no guarantee that we would  see it from a Republican or a Democrat  but it certainly sounds to me like  something at least more like the  language you'd hear from Democrats don't  you think yes but keep in mind Democrats  also want to expand a lot of other parts  of the budget entitlement spending and  that will tend to crowd out the  possibility of these programs so you  know you can debate you know which party  you prefer or social issues but I think  in the longer run it's actually going to  look fairly similar once you get past  all the rhetoric have you given any  thought to the impact of such  significant defense spending on the way  we more generally allocate resources and  the stasis and complacency that you  described partially from the idea that  we could maybe increase complacency by  knowing that we've got this massive  military industrial complex and that so  much money is tied up in bombs and  defense and so on and so forth what's  your sense about that I think people  feel quite safe they feel in a sense too  safe they're neglecting perhaps  cyberattack but what will happen is as  we continue to age it will cost more and  more money to sustain people in in their  older years which I'm all for doing but  it will mean a lot of pressure on the  defense budget  a lot of our capabilities infrastructure  you see that eroding already it's been  the case for some time  our Navy getting weaker in relative  terms and that's the likely trend it's  just kind of upkeep and innovation in a  lot of areas other than maintaining the  elderly we see those already going down  you talk about China in the book as you  described and China's circumstances are  quite different than those of the United  States have you identified any country  that is more at the level of development  or wealth that is the United States but  that has policies that you would find to  be better policies for fighting spaces  and complacency I think both Canada and  Australia have somewhat better policies  than we do those were countries that  were once once bluer than America now  they're each slightly better off and  they're better governed so we could  learn some things from them I would  start with those two places but for the  most part complacency has come to most  other wealthy countries whatwhat about  the role of healthcare I mean I know  it's interesting you mentioned Canada  and Australia both of which were  recently ranked in a study of twelve  wealthier nations more developed nations  as having some of the better health care  systems in terms of their effectiveness  they do not have the sort of employer  connected for-profit health care systems  of the United States does that play a  role here that plays a role with respect  to America I think it's one of these you  can't get there from here stories so if  we had better policy say in 1972 you  could imagine American healthcare right  now being much cheaper but given how  much we pay to think that will cut those  payments to doctors and medical devices  and hospitals it's very hard for me to  see that happening so I think we're  stuck in this box where for us truly  universal coverage would be very costly  people demand it they're not really sure  how to get it people now want more  single-payer but in terms of actually  footing the bill I don't think we're  close to any kind of consensus no no  consensus but have you seen some of the  analyses like for example by Gerald  Friedman at the University of  Massachusetts on sort of debunking or  attempting to debunk the idea that we  can't afford that I don't think we can  afford it on  lest the American public would raise its  collective hands and say well I'm  willing to accept a 5% say that tax  everything I spend whether or not they  should do that you can debate but I  don't see any willingness at all to you  know face up to those costs and say okay  let's do it  it's all thought of in terms of a free  lunch like somehow if we have  single-payer these costs will magically  fall but Medicare is a kind of  single-payer for the elderly that's  still a pretty expensive system much  more than what Canada or Australia spend  yet ensuring its elderly that's true a  very very interesting conversation and I  highly recommend the book which is of  course the complacent class the  self-defeating quest for the American  Dream economics professor Tyler Cowen is  the book's author thanks so much for  talking to us about this my pleasure  thank you
 

